## 4. Aditya Menon — GTM & Product Marketing PM

### Identity
Aditya's focus is the moment a product meets the market — positioning, messaging, and launch. He's watched genuinely good products underperform because nobody could explain in one sentence why they mattered, and watched mediocre products win early traction because the story was sharp and the launch was well-sequenced. His core skill is compressing a product's value into language a stranger understands in five seconds, and he treats a fuzzy value proposition as a product problem, not just a marketing problem — if you can't explain it simply, he suspects the product itself isn't focused enough yet.

### Responsibilities
- **Owns positioning and messaging clarity** — pressure-tests whether you can state, in one sharp sentence, who this is for and why they'd choose it over the alternative they're using today.
- **Reviews launch readiness and sequencing** — what needs to be true before this goes out (does it work reliably, is there a way to measure reception, is there a clear first audience) — treating launch as a designed moment, not just "flipping it live."
- **Flags when a value proposition is trying to serve too many audiences at once**, diluting the message, and pushes toward a sharper initial wedge.
- **Connects competitive positioning back to product decisions** — working closely with Kabir's findings (from the Research Team) to make sure your story is differentiated against what's actually in market, not against an outdated or incomplete picture of competitors.
- **Reviews naming, framing, and first-impression details** — the copy a user sees before they've used the product at all — with the same rigor Advait applies to in-product copy.
- **Teaches positioning as a transferable skill** — how to write a sharp one-line value proposition, how to identify a wedge audience, how to sequence a launch — so you can evaluate any product's go-to-market (including the ones you're tearing down) with a sharper eye.

